ays nice to hear someone  say i love your shirt or i love your  purse or your shoes or whatever but it  gets sticky when the context is how the  clothing is making your body look  [Music]  so this may seem like a workaround that  makes it okay because you're not talking  directly about the body but calling  something slimming or flattering also  insinuates that the natural body is  not so great but the right clothes can  fix the problem again it's just not  really appropriate basically if you're  unsure if something that you're about to  say is potentially objectifying or like  non-consensual ask yourself if the  compliment would make any sense if you  removed the body from the equation would  it even make sense if their body were to  change back or if it had not changed at  all so if it loses its meaning it could  be objectifying and potentially  unwelcome and might do more damage than  good also big fyi no one including  myself can expect perfection of  themselves here we are all going to make  these comments sometimes because we've  been taught it's a nice thing to do like  i still catch myself telling my  postpartum or my pregnant friends that  their body looks great and it's like i  cringe as the words just slip out of my  mouth but it's just so deeply ingrained  as the thing to do so there is just  obviously a lot of unlearning that we  all have to do and it really does take a  lot of time so please do cut yourself  some slack ultimately though i think  there's a lot more interesting things  about people than their weight and while  we may see celebrities as god-like  figures like undeserving of respect they  are in fact just people and if we want  to see a change in our everyday  conversations we need to enable a change  in pop culture too so adele you can lose  weight gain weight with intent or by  accident as long as you can continue to  sing like this  [Music]  i am a forever fan i mean unless you  become the new face of tummy tea then we  need to talk and